  "worked_solution": "We know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)={e}^{(3/4)t}.$ Thus, for $s>\\frac{3}{4},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{(3/4)t}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(s-\\frac{3}{4})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>\\frac{3}{4},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{(3/4)τ}\\,\\text{d}τ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s(s-\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{s(4s-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
